<section class="sub" id="rec-scan">
<div class="section-head">
<div class="section-num">01 · EFFECTIVE GENERAL PLAN REFORMS</div>
<h2 class="section-title">From the twenty-city reform scan</h2>
<p class="section-note">Six takeaways translated into what Santa Fe's update can act on now. See the full evidence in <a href="/research/general-plan-reforms/">Effective General Plan Reforms</a>.</p>
</div>
<ol class="patterns">
<li>
<span class="n">1</span>
<div>
<h4>Zoning change alone rarely equals construction.</h4>
<p><a href="/research/general-plan-reforms/#pcs-sacramento">Sacramento</a>'s 2026 reporting that missing middle is "still mostly missing," and <a href="/research/general-plan-reforms/#pcs-minneapolis">Minneapolis</a>'s finding that duplexes/triplexes were only ~1% of permits despite being legal everywhere, both show legalization is necessary but not sufficient. Pair zoning capacity with construction-cost and permitting-speed fixes — see <a href="/research/general-plan-reforms/#pcs-southbend">South Bend</a>'s pre-approved catalog and <a href="/research/general-plan-reforms/#pcs-spokane">Spokane</a>'s simple standards.</p>
</div>
</li>
<li>
<span class="n">2</span>
<div>
<h4>Objective, simple dimensional standards outperform discretionary review.</h4>
<p><a href="/research/general-plan-reforms/#pcs-spokane">Spokane</a>'s clean numeric standards passed cleanly and held up — clear evidence that simple, objective dimensional standards are worth adopting from the start.</p>
</div>
</li>
<li>
<span class="n">3</span>
<div>
<h4>Anchoring reform in a defensible comprehensive plan matters.</h4>
<p><a href="/research/general-plan-reforms/#pcs-gainesville">Gainesville</a>'s, <a href="/research/general-plan-reforms/#pcs-arlington">Arlington</a>'s, and <a href="/research/general-plan-reforms/#pcs-alexandria">Alexandria</a>'s experiences all show how much durability comes from how well a zoning change is grounded in an adopted plan. Doing Santa Fe's General Plan and Land Development Code together, rather than sequentially, is the more durable order of operations.</p>
</div>
</li>
<li>
<span class="n">4</span>
<div>
<h4>Corridor and node-based upzoning may fit Santa Fe better than blanket citywide change.</h4>
<p>Given the historic districts and adobe-scale character concerns, concentrating missing-middle and walkable-center intensity onto specific corridors — Cerrillos Road, St. Francis Drive, the Railyard District, South Capitol — while protecting the historic core mirrors how <a href="/research/general-plan-reforms/#pcs-bend">Bend</a> and <a href="/research/general-plan-reforms/#pcs-somerville">Somerville</a> structured their reforms.</p>
</div>
</li>
<li>
<span class="n">5</span>
<div>
<h4>Statewide legislation is doing real work elsewhere — New Mexico hasn't passed one.</h4>
<p>Arizona's HB 2721 forced <a href="/research/general-plan-reforms/#pcs-flagstaff">Flagstaff</a>'s hand; Oregon's rules did the same for <a href="/research/general-plan-reforms/#pcs-bend">Bend</a> and <a href="/research/general-plan-reforms/#pcs-portland">Portland</a>; Colorado's 2024 package moved <a href="/research/general-plan-reforms/#pcs-boulder">Boulder</a>. Without comparable state legislation, Santa Fe's reform needs its own political coalition — closer to the <a href="/research/general-plan-reforms/#pcs-missoula">Missoula</a> and <a href="/research/general-plan-reforms/#pcs-charlotte">Charlotte</a> experience than the Flagstaff/Bend one.</p>
</div>
</li>
<li>
<span class="n">6</span>
<div>
<h4>Engagement design predicts durability.</h4>
<p><a href="/research/general-plan-reforms/#pcs-missoula">Missoula</a>'s deliberate resident-involvement process and <a href="/research/general-plan-reforms/#pcs-flagstaff">Flagstaff</a>'s broad-support hearings show what's possible: front-loading engagement, before a draft exists, correlates strongly with reforms that stick.</p>
</div>
</li>
</ol>
</section>
<section class="sub" id="rec-guide">
<div class="section-head">
<div class="section-num">02 · GENERAL PLAN LANGUAGE COMPARISONS</div>
<h2 class="section-title">From the verbatim plan-language review</h2>
<p class="section-note">Drawing on the sharpest language across the twenty plans reviewed, adapted to what Santa Fe's update is positioned to achieve. See the full evidence in <a href="/research/plan-language/">General Plan Language Comparisons</a>.</p>
</div>
<ol class="patterns">
<li>
<span class="n">1</span>
<div>
<h4>Number every goal and pair it with a named mechanism, not just a value statement.</h4>
<p><a href="/research/plan-language/#glg-minneapolis">Minneapolis</a>, <a href="/research/plan-language/#glg-charlotte">Charlotte</a>, <a href="/research/plan-language/#glg-tacoma">Tacoma</a>, and <a href="/research/plan-language/#glg-missoula">Missoula</a> number every policy and pair it with a named action step or code section; softer language (<a href="/research/plan-language/#glg-boulder">Boulder</a>'s center policies) leans on "encourage" and "in appropriate locations." Numbering and naming a mechanism for every policy is the single highest-leverage move available to Santa Fe's update.</p>
</div>
</li>
<li>
<span class="n">2</span>
<div>
<h4>Name and map Santa Fe's own centers and corridors explicitly.</h4>
<p>Don't leave "activity center" or "mixed-use node" undefined — candidates already visible in local discussion include the Railyard District, Cerrillos Road, St. Francis Drive, and South Capitol. <a href="/research/plan-language/#glg-austin">Austin</a>'s 48 geocoded centers, <a href="/research/plan-language/#glg-tacoma">Tacoma</a>'s tiered hierarchy with per-tier density numbers, and <a href="/research/plan-language/#glg-somerville">Somerville</a>'s five named "transformative areas" all show what that looks like once it's a specific place on a map with a specific standard attached, not just an adjective.</p>
</div>
</li>
<li>
<span class="n">3</span>
<div>
<h4>State that land-use designations will map onto specific LDC zoning districts.</h4>
<p>The way <a href="/research/plan-language/#glg-charlotte">Charlotte</a>'s plan does — since Santa Fe is fortunately doing both documents at once, this turns the General Plan and Land Development Code from two documents that might drift apart into one continuous chain of authority.</p>
</div>
</li>
<li>
<span class="n">4</span>
<div>
<h4>Adopt one simple, dated, testable walkability standard.</h4>
<p>A "10-minute" or "15-minute neighborhood" access test, as a signature, trackable commitment.</p>
</div>
</li>
<li>
<span class="n">5</span>
<div>
<h4>Set a small number of numeric, dated targets.</h4>
<p>Housing units, % affordable, % of growth directed to named centers — and commit to a public tracking dashboard, as <a href="/research/plan-language/#glg-somerville">Somerville</a> does. <a href="/research/plan-language/#glg-tacoma">Tacoma</a>'s "42,865 units by 2044" and <a href="/research/plan-language/#glg-missoula">Missoula</a>'s "22,000–27,500 units by 2045" show what a falsifiable, dated target looks like in practice.</p>
</div>
</li>
<li>
<span class="n">6</span>
<div>
<h4>Resolve the historic-character-vs-housing tension with one concrete tool.</h4>
<p><a href="/research/plan-language/#glg-santabarbara">Santa Barbara</a>'s unit-size density formula and <a href="/research/plan-language/#glg-flagstaff">Flagstaff</a>'s transect code both let a historic-scaled building actually contain more housing units, rather than leaving staff to referee "preserve character" against "increase housing" case by case.</p>
</div>
</li>
<li>
<span class="n">7</span>
<div>
<h4>Keep the vision statement warm if that fits Santa Fe's voice.</h4>
<p>The evidence says that's not where plans succeed or fail — <a href="/research/plan-language/#glg-missoula">Missoula</a>'s and <a href="/research/plan-language/#glg-minneapolis">Minneapolis</a>'s vision paragraphs aren't meaningfully more specific than 1990s comp-plan boilerplate. What changed is everything below the vision statement; spend the specificity budget on the goals and implementation sections instead.</p>
</div>
</li>
</ol>
</section>
<section class="sub" id="rec-incentives">
<div class="section-head">
<div class="section-num">03 · AFFORDABLE HOUSING INCENTIVES</div>
<h2 class="section-title">From the state &amp; city incentive programs</h2>
<p class="section-note">Six takeaways translated into what Santa Fe's update can act on now. See the full evidence in <a href="/research/housing-incentives/">Affordable Housing Incentives</a>.</p>
</div>
<ol class="patterns">
<li>
<span class="n">1</span>
<div>
<h4>Make the incentive automatic, not discretionary.</h4>
<p><a href="/research/housing-incentives/#ahs-california">California</a>'s SB 35 and <a href="/research/housing-incentives/#ahc-austin">Austin</a>'s Affordability Unlocked both waive the rezoning-and-hearing process entirely for qualifying projects — a developer who meets the objective standard gets the approval, no vote required. That's a real part of why both produced fast, measurable results.</p>
</div>
</li>
<li>
<span class="n">2</span>
<div>
<h4>Legalize the smallest unit first.</h4>
<p><a href="/research/housing-incentives/#ahs-california">California</a>'s decade-long ADU law series turned backyard and internal units into the state's single biggest housing-production story — permits up over 150-fold since 2016, reaching 19% of all new California housing by 2022. The smallest, cheapest-to-permit unit type is often the highest-leverage place to start.</p>
</div>
</li>
<li>
<span class="n">3</span>
<div>
<h4>A property-tax incentive can activate land that's already zoned.</h4>
<p><a href="/research/housing-incentives/#ahc-minneapolis">Minneapolis</a>'s 4(d) program and <a href="/research/housing-incentives/#ahs-washington">Washington</a>'s Multifamily Tax Exemption don't add any new zoning capacity at all — they make building on capacity that already exists pencil out financially, which is often the real barrier standing between an adopted plan and a built building.</p>
</div>
</li>
<li>
<span class="n">4</span>
<div>
<h4>Tie the incentive to location, not just to affordability.</h4>
<p><a href="/research/housing-incentives/#ahs-washington">Washington</a>'s new transit-station MFTE tier and <a href="/research/housing-incentives/#ahc-austin">Austin</a>'s transit criterion for S.M.A.R.T. Housing both link the tax or fee benefit specifically to walkable, transit-connected sites. That link is the mechanism Santa Fe would need to get compact, low-car-dependence outcomes specifically — not just more units in any location.</p>
</div>
</li>
<li>
<span class="n">5</span>
<div>
<h4>Expect uneven uptake, and plan for it.</h4>
<p><a href="/research/housing-incentives/#ahs-california">California</a>'s SB 9 duplex/lot-split law saw only 266 permits statewide as of 2023 against 6.1 million eligible parcels, while the ADU laws next to it produced explosive growth. An enabling law's real-world success depends on whether the economics pencil out for a typical property owner, not just on the legal permission existing.</p>
</div>
</li>
<li>
<span class="n">6</span>
<div>
<h4>Pair state funding with local capacity — not in place of it.</h4>
<p><a href="/research/housing-incentives/#ahs-colorado">Colorado</a>'s Proposition 123 and <a href="/research/housing-incentives/#ahs-minnesota">Minnesota</a>'s Local Affordable Housing Aid work because they layer money on top of jurisdictions that also have the zoning capacity to build. Funding without buildable capacity, or capacity without funding, both tend to stall.</p>
</div>
</li>
</ol>
</section>
<section class="sub" id="rec-ldc">
<div class="section-head">
<div class="section-num">04 · LAND DEVELOPMENT CODE CONCEPTS</div>
<h2 class="section-title">From the seven code-reform concepts</h2>
<p class="section-note">What these concepts suggest for Santa Fe's own code rewrite. See the full evidence in <a href="/research/ldc-concepts/">Land Development Code Concepts</a>.</p>
</div>
<ol class="patterns">
<li>
<span class="n">1</span>
<div>
<h4>Fill in the blank dimensional-standards table with objective numbers.</h4>
<p>Santa Fe's own code currently leaves its dimensional standards table (§14-6.5(D)) blank — the same gap Effective General Plan Reforms flags <a href="/cities/spokane/">Spokane</a>'s table as a model for. Objective, numeric standards are also the prerequisite for any of the by-right approval below.</p>
</div>
</li>
<li>
<span class="n">2</span>
<div>
<h4>Use the General Plan and Land Development Code rewrite to consolidate, not just update.</h4>
<p>Santa Fe is already touching both documents at once — a rare opportunity to merge them into one internally consistent code, the way <a href="/cities/charlotte/">Charlotte</a>, <a href="/cities/columbus/">Columbus</a>, and Louisville have, rather than updating each on its own separate track.</p>
</div>
</li>
<li>
<span class="n">3</span>
<div>
<h4>Adopt a pre-approved plan set for the smallest, most common unit types.</h4>
<p>A catalog of pre-approved ADU, casita, and small-lot infill plans — sized to Santa Fe's own historic-scale patterns — would cut permitting time and design cost precisely where character concerns are most sensitive to case-by-case review.</p>
</div>
</li>
<li>
<span class="n">4</span>
<div>
<h4>Route routine quasi-judicial decisions to an independent reviewer.</h4>
<p>Minor variances and conditional-use permits don't need a full council hearing every time. Washington State's hearing-examiner model frees elected bodies to focus on policy-level decisions rather than case-by-case disputes.</p>
</div>
</li>
<li>
<span class="n">5</span>
<div>
<h4>Attach a decision deadline to any new by-right or objective-standard track.</h4>
<p>An objective standard only delivers a fast yes if the code also requires a timely answer. A shot-clock provision — modeled on California's 60-day ADU deadline — makes "meets the standard" actually mean "gets approved on time."</p>
</div>
</li>
<li>
<span class="n">6</span>
<div>
<h4>Where discretionary design review stays, write its criteria in numeric terms.</h4>
<p>For the larger or more sensitive projects where Santa Fe keeps a discretionary review step, defining that review's criteria as measurable numbers — rather than open-ended compatibility language — can protect character without the multi-year delays documented in cities that rely on subjective board review.</p>
</div>
</li>
</ol>
</section>
<section class="sub" id="rec-historic">
<div class="section-head">
<div class="section-num">05 · HISTORIC PRESERVATION APPROACHES</div>
<h2 class="section-title">From the eight preservation case studies</h2>
<p class="section-note">These recommendations are organized around the four gaps Santa Fe's own 2021 <em>Continuity and Connection</em> pre-planning report identifies in its historic-preservation program. The Governing Body has not formally acted on that report, so these gaps remain open input for this update rather than a settled prior decision. See the full evidence, including that report's findings, in <a href="/research/historic-preservation/">Historic Preservation Approaches</a>.</p>
</div>
<ol class="patterns">
<li>
<span class="n">1</span>
<div>
<h4>Pair preservation with financial tools that answer the displacement tension in General Plan Policy 2-G-5 directly — not only through design review.</h4>
<p>Policy 2-G-5 (and Implementing Policy 2-5) already name the tension between preserving historic fabric and enabling affordable housing, but naming a tension isn't a tool for resolving it. <a href="/research/historic-preservation/#hp-miami">Miami</a>'s transfer-of-development-rights program lets a historic building's foregone density get built elsewhere nearby, so preservation doesn't have to mean a net loss of housing capacity. <a href="/research/historic-preservation/#hp-sanfrancisco">San Francisco</a>'s Legacy Business Registry answers a different half of the same problem — it funds the rent and operating costs of long-standing businesses directly, rather than only protecting the buildings they happen to occupy. Santa Fe likely needs both: a property-side tool and a tenancy-side tool.</p>
</div>
</li>
<li>
<span class="n">2</span>
<div>
<h4>Restart systematic survey work with a modern methodology, and use it to broaden whose heritage gets documented.</h4>
<p>Santa Fe's systematic historic-resources surveys stopped after 2002. <a href="/research/historic-preservation/#hp-losangeles">Los Angeles</a>'s SurveyLA shows what a modern, citywide restart looks like: a tablet-based field survey system, a citywide historic context statement, and — notably — dedicated context studies for communities a narrower, architecture-first survey would likely miss. That last piece matters because Santa Fe's own preservation framework centers a single "Santa Fe Style" the report describes as a "synthetic combination" of building traditions from around 1912; a modernized survey is the moment to widen that lens rather than re-document the same buildings again.</p>
</div>
</li>
<li>
<span class="n">3</span>
<div>
<h4>Make sure a preservation commission's recommendation can't be silently dropped.</h4>
<p><a href="/research/historic-preservation/#hp-charlotte">Charlotte</a>'s Steele Creek Manse was demolished after its Historic Landmarks Commission recommended landmark status but city staff never forwarded that recommendation to Council for a vote. Santa Fe's report flags the Historic Districts Review Board's authority relative to Council, and the lack of a formal definition of "historic significance," as unresolved. Whatever the resolution, the path from a board's recommendation to a Council decision should be a required step, not a discretionary one.</p>
</div>
</li>
<li>
<span class="n">4</span>
<div>
<h4>Favor automatic, self-executing incentives over competitive grant programs.</h4>
<p><a href="/research/historic-preservation/#hp-charlotte">Charlotte</a>'s 50% property-tax deferral applies automatically on landmark designation, with no separate application or award process. An incentive owners don't have to compete for is one more owners will actually use.</p>
</div>
</li>
<li>
<span class="n">5</span>
<div>
<h4>Consider a tiered conservation-district option below full landmark status.</h4>
<p><a href="/research/historic-preservation/#hp-sanantonio">San Antonio</a>'s Neighborhood Conservation Districts offer a middle option between no protection and full historic-district design review — useful where a neighborhood has real character worth protecting but not the density of contributing structures a full historic district requires.</p>
</div>
</li>
<li>
<span class="n">6</span>
<div>
<h4>A privately funded revolving fund is worth considering as a longer-term addition.</h4>
<p><a href="/research/historic-preservation/#hp-savannah">Savannah</a>'s revolving fund lets preservation pay for itself over time rather than relying solely on regulation or public subsidy — it doesn't require immediate action, but it's worth keeping on Santa Fe's longer-term toolkit list alongside the displacement-focused tools above.</p>
</div>
</li>
<li>
<span class="n">7</span>
<div>
<h4>Monitor tourism and short-term-rental pressure on the historic core proactively, not reactively.</h4>
<p><a href="/research/historic-preservation/#hp-charleston">Charleston</a> and <a href="/research/historic-preservation/#hp-savannah">Savannah</a> both show that a successful, well-funded preservation program does not by itself resolve the population and quality-of-life pressure that preservation-driven tourism can create. Santa Fe's own historic core carries a comparable tourism economy, and tracking this pressure before it becomes acute is cheaper than responding to it afterward.</p>
</div>
</li>
<li>
<span class="n">8</span>
<div>
<h4>Close the two items New Mexico's own CLG standards flag as certification requirements, not just good practice.</h4>
<p>Santa Fe is a Certified Local Government, and New Mexico's CLG Manual sets two of its standards as actual certification requirements rather than suggestions: a regularly updated resources survey (suggested every 5–10 years; Santa Fe's has gone 24) and professional-credential seats on the review board (at least two members meeting the Secretary of the Interior's Professional Qualifications Standards, "to the extent… available in the community"). Both map directly onto items 2 and 3 above. See the full <a href="/research/historic-preservation/#hp-standards-group">standards comparison</a> for the design-guideline and demolition-review items the manual leaves to local discretion, which are lower priority for exactly that reason.</p>
</div>
</li>
</ol>
</section>
